<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Government of India Act 1919</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1919">1919</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> Act 1919 was a British law that put into effect the Montagu–Chelmsford reforms promised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montagu-declaration">Montagu Declaration</a> of 1917. It introduced dyarchy in the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, transferring some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> such as education and health to ministers responsible to elected legislatures while key subjects stayed with the governor. It also enlarged the electorate and created a bicameral central legislature.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The act of the Parliament of the United Kingdom gave effect to the reforms recommended in the report of Edwin Montagu, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, and the Viceroy, Chelmsford, and was meant to expand the participation of Indians in their government. It received royal assent on 23 December 1919. On the same day the King-Emperor issued a proclamation that reviewed earlier legislation for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, from the Acts of 1773 and 1784 to that of 1909, and said the new act entrusted the elected representatives of the people with a definite share in the government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>A preamble declared that the aim of British policy was the gradual introduction of responsible government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the first act to say so. In the provinces, the transferred subjects, among them agriculture, local government, health and education, went to Indian ministers who could be removed by a vote of no confidence, while law and order, finance, land revenue and irrigation stayed with the governor and his executive council. Income tax remained a central source of revenue, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> were assigned 25 per cent of it.</p><p>At the centre the Legislative Assembly had 145 members, 104 of them elected, with three-year terms, and the Council of State 60 members, 34 elected, with five-year terms. No bill passed without the Viceroy's assent, and he could enact a bill without the legislature. Three of the eight members of his Executive Council had to be Indians. The act also created a Public Service Commission, extended separate representation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, Anglo-Indians and Europeans, and provided for a statutory commission after ten years, which became the Simon Commission of 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> rejected the act as inadequate, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Bal Gangadhar Tilak</a> called the reforms a sunless dawn. Other leaders accepted it: Surendranath Banerjee and Tej Bahadur Sapru formed the Indian Liberal Federation. Congress boycotted the first elections under the act in 1921, and the Swaraj Party, founded in 1923 by Motilal Nehru and Chittaranjan Das, sought to obstruct the legislature from within. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rowlatt-act">Rowlatt Act</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jallianwala-bagh-massacre">Jallianwala Bagh massacre</a> of April 1919, in the same year, undermined Indian confidence in the reforms. What remained of the act after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1935">Government of India Act 1935</a> was repealed in 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Government of India Act 1919” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
